   "text": {
    "L1": "Plants move two important materials through different tissues.",
    "L2": "Roots absorb water and dissolved minerals from the soil, mainly through root hairs.",
    "L3": "Xylem carries this water and these minerals upward through the stem to the leaves.",
    "L4": "Water leaving the leaves through stomata is called transpiration, and it helps pull more water upward.",
    "L5": "Leaves make food, and phloem carries that food to all parts of the plant.",
    "L6": "Food can move upward to buds and fruits, or downward to roots and storage organs.",
    "L7": "So the myth is wrong: water moves up in xylem, while food moves in phloem in both directions.",
    "L8": "Now choose the tissue that carries food from leaves to a growing fruit."
   },
